## 11. Critical assumptions, unsupported cases, risks

1. `[ASSUMPTION]` The 3 GB/16 GB envelope is achievable with tile-cache design (W3 arithmetic says yes; W4 must confirm with real I/O — if decompression dominates, fall back to precomputed pyramid levels as mandatory for sources > 1 GB).
2. `[UNSUPPORTED v0]` Vendor-native formats and WSI (§6.2) — fail closed; original metadata retained. Registration, segmentation, cloud — out of scope (leads).
3. `[UNSUPPORTED v0]` Concurrent multi-writer editing of one bundle on shared storage — last-writer-wins on manifest with pre-rename backup; documented as unsafe; single-writer-per-project etiquette (leads L7 for real concurrency later). `[INFERENCE]` This matches the brief's "exchange bundles" model rather than shared-live editing.
4. `[RISK]` Calibration trust: precedents show files lie about pixel size [S11, S04]; our uncalibrated-state design reduces but cannot remove wrong-number risk; mitigation = prominent scale bar + one-click "show raw metadata".
5. `[RISK]` OME-Zarr/NGFF evolves (0.5/0.6 exist [S20/S21]); we pin 0.4 for v0 reads/writes of derived data and version-stamp everything; 0.5+ adoption is a lead (L5).
6. `[RISK]` Windows/macOS atomic-rename semantics: `os.replace` is atomic on POSIX and Windows same-volume; cross-volume moves are staged same-volume first `[INFERENCE]` (standard practice; platform test included in W5).
7. `[LIMIT]` All executed checks (W1–W3) are isolated arithmetic/logic component checks; they do not establish behavior of napari, QuPath, tifffile, or any built application.

## 12. Opportunity and alternatives

**Opportunity (recommended to adopt early): make derived artifacts NGFF-0.4 OME-Zarr.** `[FACT]` tifffile already speaks zarr/NGFF [S04], QuPath 0.7.0 imports zarr images [S02], napari reads zarr natively [S05]. `[INFERENCE]` Storing pyramids/masks/derived data as NGFF-0.4 costs little in v0 (the writer is a chunked-directory writer + a `.zattrs` we already schema) and buys: free interop for reviewers using existing tools, a path to cloud later, and one format to test instead of two. This is the single highest-leverage source-backed choice in the plan.

**Plausible alternative (recorded, not chosen for v0): embed napari as the canvas** instead of building our own tile compositor. Pros: instant multiscale/lazy rendering, coordinate-transform and annotation layers already built [S01, S05]; Cons: Qt-binding and dependency weight per workstation, UI/UX constrained by napari's layer model, project-persistence and export contracts still ours to build anyway, and long-term API churn (monthly releases, "Meso" breaking-change class [S01]). `[CHOICE]` Keep as L1: revisit if the W4 benchmark shows CPU compositing cannot meet responsiveness.

**Alternative also recorded**: single-file SQLite project container (atomic transactions out of the box) — rejected for v0 because per-entry side files (annotation JSONL, zarr chunks, sidecars) keep the bundle diffable/syncable over shared storage and avoid DB corruption handling for large blobs; revisit if journal compaction becomes a burden.

